ABSTRACT: In this poster, we present the construction of HGDAVE (Heterogeneous Generative Dataset for Unmanned Autonomous Systems), a new dataset for Connected and Autonomous Vehicles (CAVs) and Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), namely Unmanned Autonomous Systems (UASes). The dataset will be used to train artificial intelligence (AI) models to detect cybersecurity and safety-related risks, malfunctions, and crashes. The dataset was collected from three sources: 1) script-generated flying or driving missions, 2) software fuzzer-generated crashes instances, and 3) cybersecurity exploits generated by ethical hackers. To collect the data, we utilized the Digital Twin (DT) to replicate the behavior of UASes, which provides data that can be used to analyze, develop, and detect new anomaly detection algorithms. © 2023 IEEE.

[R0343] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings - 2026 IEEE 50th Annual Computers, Software, and Applications Conference, COMPSAC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: PentestLLM: A Cyber Kill Chain-driven Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework Enabling Automatic Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cyber Kill Chain; Large Language Model; Multi-Agent; Penetration Testing; Prompt Engineering; Automatic testing; Chains; Intelligent agents; Large scale systems; Network security; Security systems; Agentic AI; Cybe kill chain; Kill chain; Language model; Large language model; Modelling framework; Multi agent; Penetration testing; Proactive security; Prompt engineering; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is a proactive security assessment technique. Traditional pentesting approaches often rely on manual operations, which are time-consuming, laborious, and prone to high rates of false positives and negatives. In order to address such an arduous challenge, this paper introduces PentestLLM, a novel framework leveraging the capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) to automate the pentest process. PentestLLM facilitates an efficient pentesting process by integrating cuttingedge natural language processing techniques with the cyber kill chain (CKC) approach to orchestrate the task workflows efficiently. The PentestLLM framework is designed to be adaptable, learning from each interaction to improve its effectiveness over time. Therefore by harnessing the power of LLMs, PentestLLM can understand and interpret complex system configurations and security policies. As a result, it can perform sophisticated attack simulations and produce actionable insights at a scale and speed beyond the capabilities of human experts alone. A proof-ofconcept has been developed for the PentestLLM associating with real-world LLMs. Based on the implementation, a number of experiments have been conducted. PentestLLM's performance is evaluated through extensive experiments across various environments, demonstrating its ability to accurately identify vulnerabilities and generate practical exploitations. PentestLLM represents a significant advancement in automated pentesting over prior work, demonstrating its potential as a powerful tool for enabling proactive defense strategies. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0344] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 5th International Conference on AI in Cybersecurity, ICAIC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: A Systematic Security Analysis of Model Context Protocol: Vulnerabilities, Exploits, and Mitigations
KEYWORDS: Configuration Vulnerabilities; Context Injection Attacks; Model Context Protocol (MCP); Resource Exploitation; Tool Manipulation; Computer crime; Security systems; Configuration vulnerability; Context injection attack; Language model; Model context protocol; Model contexts; Protocol implementation; Resource exploitation; Security analysis; Systematic security; Tool manipulation; Network security
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) gain significantly enhanced capabilities through the Model Context Protocol (MCP), which allows them to connect directly with external tools and services. However, this expanded functionality introduces previously unexamined security vulnerabilities. Our study provides the first thorough security assessment of MCP implementations, uncovering serious weaknesses in filesystem operations, database connections, and API integrations. We conducted systematic penetration testing on 15 different MCP server implementations and successfully executed attacks including directory traversal, SQL injection, credential theft, and resource exhaustion. Our findings show that 87% of examined MCP servers have at least one critical security flaw, while 34% are vulnerable to full system takeover. We develop a detailed classification system for MCP security threats and introduce effective defensive measures that decrease successful attack rates by as much as 94%. Our research lays the groundwork for secure MCP implementation and underscores the essential importance of prioritizing security when designing LLM integration protocols. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0345] year=2026 | venue=Lecture Notes in Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Adversary: Red-Teaming in the Age of LLM
KEYWORDS: Cyber-Agent; Cybersecurity; Language Model Agent; Red-teaming; Autonomous agents; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Action plan; Attack planning; Cybe-agent; Cyber security; Language model; Language model agent; Lateral movement; Model agents; Multisteps; Red teaming; Benchmarking
ABSTRACT: Language Model Agents (LMAs) are emerging as a powerful primitive for augmenting red-team operations. They can support attack planning, adversary emulation, and the orchestration of multi-step activity such as lateral movement, a core enabling capability of advanced persistent threat (APT) campaigns. Using frameworks such as MITRE ATT&CK, we analyze where these agents intersect with core offensive functions and assess current strengths and limitations of LMAs with an emphasis on governance and realistic evaluation. We benchmark LMAs across two lateral-movement scenarios in a controlled adversary-emulation environment, where LMAs interact with instrumented cyber agents, observe execution artifacts, and iteratively adapt based on environmental feedback. Each scenario is formalized as an ordered task chain with explicit validation predicates, leveraging an LLM-as-a-Judge paradigm to ensure deterministic outcome verification. We compare three operational modalities: fully autonomous execution, self-scaffolded planning, and expert-defined action plan. Preliminary findings indicate that expert-defined action plans yield higher task-completion rates relative to other operational modes. However, failure remains frequent across all modalities, largely attributable to brittle command invocation, environmental and deployment instability, and recurring errors in credential management and state handling. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.

[R0346] year=2026 | venue=CEUR Workshop Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Architecture of AI-agent orchestration for automated testing and cyber threat analysis in DevSecOps environments
KEYWORDS: AI-agent orchestration; automated testing; cloud-native infrastructures; cyber threat analysis; DevSecOps; large language models; Anomaly detection; Architecture; Automation; Cloud computing architecture; Cybersecurity; Decision making; Distributed cloud; Embedded systems; Intelligent agents; Memory architecture; Network security; Pipelines; Telemetering; Telemetering equipment; AI-agent orchestration; Automated testing; Cloud-native infrastructure; Cybe threat analyze; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Devsecop; Language model; Large language model; Threats analysis; Integration testing
ABSTRACT: The rapid growth of cloud-native infrastructures, continuous integration and continuous deployment pipelines, and distributed DevSecOps ecosystems has significantly increased the complexity of automated software testing and cybersecurity monitoring processes. Traditional QA systems and centralized orchestration approaches often demonstrate limited adaptability to dynamic deployment environments, high-frequency telemetry streams, and rapidly evolving cyber threats. This study proposes an architecture of AI-agent orchestration for automated testing and cyber threat analysis in DevSecOps environments based on coordinated interaction between intelligent agents and large language models. The proposed architecture integrates distributed AI agents responsible for automated testing, telemetry analysis, vulnerability detection, anomaly monitoring, and contextual decision-making within cloud-native CI/CD infrastructures. The methodology combines telemetry-driven anomaly detection, LLM-assisted code and infrastructure analysis, adaptive task prioritization, and asynchronous orchestration mechanisms for distributed cybersecurity operations. Experimental evaluation was conducted in a Kubernetes-based DevSecOps environment using Docker containerization, GitHub Actions pipelines, distributed telemetry collection mechanisms, and simulated cyberattack scenarios. The obtained results demonstrated improved threat detection accuracy, reduced orchestration latency, adaptive workload redistribution, and enhanced scalability compared to traditional QA pipelines and single-agent cybersecurity systems. The proposed orchestration architecture achieved a Threat Detection Rate of 96.8% while maintaining stable operation under dynamically changing cloud workloads and high-frequency telemetry conditions. The developed methodology can be applied to intelligent QA automation, adaptive cybersecurity monitoring, and distributed threat analysis in modern cloud-native DevSecOps infrastructures. © 2026 Copyright for this paper by its authors.

[R0347] year=2025 | venue=IEEE International Conference on Data Mining Workshops, ICDMW | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SoK: Measuring what Matters for Closed-Loop Security Agents
KEYWORDS: autonomous pentesting; closed-loop security; knowledge graphs; LLM agents; patching; reconnaisance; root cause analysis; security; security agents; systematization of knowledge; Agents; Artificial intelligence; Benchmarking; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Life cycle; Security systems; Verification; Autonomous pentesting; Closed-loop; Closed-loop security; Knowledge graphs; LLM agent; Patching; Reconnaisance; Root cause analysis; Security; Security agents; Systematisation; Systematization of knowledge; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity is a relentless arms race, with AI driven offensive systems evolving faster than traditional defenses can adapt. Research and tooling remain fragmented across isolated defensive functions, creating blind spots that adversaries exploit. Autonomous agents capable of integrating, exploit confirmation, remediation, and validation into a single closed loop offer promise, but the field lacks three essentials: a framework defining the agentic capabilities of security systems across security life cycle, a principled method for evaluating closed loop agents, and a benchmark for measuring their performance in practice. We introduce CLASP-the Closed-Loop Autonomous Security Performance framework which aligns the security lifecycle (reconnaissance, exploitation, root cause analysis, patch synthesis, validation) with core agentic capabilities (planning, tool use, memory, reasoning, reflection & perception) providing a common vocabulary and rubric for assessing agentic capabilities in security tasks. By applying CLASP to 21 representative works, we map where systems demonstrate strengths, and where capability gaps persist. We then define the Closed-Loop Capability (CLC) Score, a composite metric quantifying both degree of loop closure and operational effectiveness, and outline the requirements for a closed loop benchmark. Together, CLASP and the CLC Score, provide the vocabulary, diagnostics, and measurements needed to advance both function level performance and measure closed loop security agents. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0348] year=2025 | venue=CEUR Workshop Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SecFlow: An Agentic LLM-Based Framework for Modular Cyberattack Analysis and Explainability
KEYWORDS: Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Cyber Threat Detection; Fine-tuned Security Classifiers; Intrusion Detection Systems; Large Language Models; Application programs; Classification (of information); Computation theory; Computer crime; Intelligent agents; Intrusion detection; Network security; Software testing; Agentic retrieval-augmented generation; Cybe threat detection; Cyber threats; Cyber-attacks; Fine-tuned security classifier; Intrusion Detection Systems; Language model; Large language model; Modulars; Threat detection; Decision making
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) are rapidly reshaping the software engineering landscape, with applications ranging from code synthesis to intelligent assistance in debugging and testing. In this work, we propose SecFlow, a modular agentic framework designed for intelligent classification and contextual explanation of web-based cyberattacks. Unlike traditional detection pipelines, SecFlow integrates fine-tuned LLM-based classifiers and a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) component into a cohesive architecture, enabling contextualized reasoning and transparent report generation from raw logs. We focus on a single attack vector, Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI), to illustrate the benefits of a dynamic and explainable security pipeline. The system autonomously identifies malicious payloads, retrieves relevant domain knowledge, and produces human-readable reports detailing the nature of the attack, its potential impact, and recommended mitigation strategies. Extensive evaluations show that our approach outperforms monolithic models in accuracy, robustness, and interpretability. Furthermore, the architecture is fully extensible, allowing integration with existing security infrastructures and future inclusion of additional classifiers or automated defense routines. Our findings highlight that LLMs, even without directly generating code, can serve as reliable reasoning agents in software workflows where transparency, explainability, and modular decision-making are critical. © 2025 Copyright for this paper by its authors.

[R0349] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - IEEE Military Communications Conference MILCOM | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Automating Cybersecurity Risk Assessment for AI Systems in Military & Critical Infrastructure
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Cyber Defense; Risk Assessment; Software-Defined Defense; Artificial intelligence; Critical infrastructures; Cybersecurity; Embedded systems; Network security; Public works; Artificial intelligence systems; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Hybrid artificial intelligences; Intelligence models; Network-centric; Risk assessment framework; Risks assessments; Software-defined defense; Risk assessment
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity risk assessment frameworks for critical infrastructure have traditionally focused on network-centric vulnerabilities, with limited integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI)-specific risks. As hybrid AI models are increasingly embedded in Autonomous Cyber Defense (ACD) systems, a standardized approach to evaluate both network and AI components is needed. This paper proposes a framework for automating cybersecurity risk assessment that jointly considers conventional infrastructure assets and AI models. It extends established frameworks by introducing structured asset taxonomies, fuzzy risk metrics, and AI-specific threat modeling dimensions. The approach is designed to support Software-Defined Defense (SDD), which implements interfaces to monitor and control multi-layer military systems across strategic, deployed, and tactical domains. The framework integrates Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI), vulnerability databases (CVE/CWE), and adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) into an automated workflow, enabling risk reasoning over both AI and traditional network assets. Empirical validation using two use cases, backdoor attack and LLM poisoning, demonstrates the framework's ability to assess evolving risks in mission-critical infrastructures using AI systems. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0350] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Big Data, BigData | type=Conference paper
TITLE: IRSDA: An Agent-Orchestrated Framework for Enterprise Intrusion Response
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cybersecurity; Digital Assistant; Intrusion Response System; Knowledge Graph; Large Language Model (LLM); Artificial intelligence; Autonomous agents; Behavioral research; Cybersecurity; Distributed computer systems; Intrusion detection; Knowledge management; Network security; Agentic AI; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Decisions makings; Digital assistants; Intrusion response; Intrusion response system; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; Decision making
ABSTRACT: Modern enterprise systems face escalating cyber threats that are increasingly dynamic, distributed, and multi-stage in nature. Traditional intrusion detection and response systems often rely on static rules and manual workflows, which limit their ability to respond with the speed and precision required in high-stakes environments. To address these challenges, we present the Intrusion Response System Digital Assistant (IRSDA), an agent-based framework designed to deliver autonomous and policy-compliant cyber defense. IRSDA combines Self-Adaptive Autonomic Computing Systems (SA-ACS) with the Knowledge guided Monitor, Analyze, Plan, and Execute (MAPE-K) loop to support real-time, partition-aware decision-making across enterprise infrastructure. IRSDA incorporates a knowledge-driven architecture that integrates contextual information with AI-based reasoning to support system-guided intrusion response. The framework leverages retrieval mechanisms and structured representations to inform decision-making while maintaining alignment with operational policies. We assess the system using a representative real-world microservices application, demonstrating its ability to automate containment, enforce compliance, and provide traceable outputs for security analyst interpretation. This work outlines a modular and agent-driven approach to cyber defense that emphasizes explainability, system-state awareness, and operational control in intrusion response. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0351] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of IEEE International Conference on Modelling, Simulation and Intelligent Computing, MoSICom 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Examining the Factors Influencing Generative AI's Impact on Quantum Cybersecurity: A Thematic Analysis
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Demotivators; Generative AI; Motivators; Quantum Computing; Thematic Analysis; Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; Decision making; Quantum cryptography; Quantum optics; Applications domains; Cyber security; Demotivator; Driving forces; Effective mechanisms; Generative AI; Motivator; Quantum Computing; Risks and uncertainties; Thematic analysis; Quantum computers
ABSTRACT: The rapid advancement of generative AI and its integration with quantum cybersecurity present significant opportunities and new problems. While generative AI can improve security in quantum computing, it also brings new risks and uncertainties. Discovering the driving forces or constraints of using generative A I in this application domain is essential to devising effective mechanisms for harnessing its possibilities while minimizing the risks. The main objectives of this paper are to perform a thematic analysis on several primary motivators and demotivators driving the adoption and impact of generative AI in quantum cybersecurity. The study found several key motivators, such as the potential for new predictive capabilities, the automation of threat detection, and the strengthening of quantum encryption protocols. The sources of demotivation included suspicion about the dependability of AI models in a super complex quantum world, ethical problems of the autonomous decision-making AI, and node computational power to integrate AI into quantum. These actors are identified to play an essential role in the feasibility and acceptability of generative AI for quantum cybersecurity. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0352] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE International Conference on AI and Data Analytics, ICAD 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: From Scripts to Prompts: How Large Language Models Are Transforming Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: AI Agent; Ethical Hacking; Large Language Models; LLM; Offensive Security; Penetration Testing; Autonomous agents; Behavioral research; Costs; Ethical aspects; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Open systems; Reliability analysis; Virtual reality; AI agent; Ethical hacking; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Offensive security; Penetration testing; Testing framework; Economic and social effects
ABSTRACT: Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have introduced new opportunities for automating of Penetration testing (PT) tasks. However, existing LLM-based frameworks continue to struggle with unstable long-horizon reasoning, context drift, inconsistent command generation, and limited ethical control. To address these challenges, this paper presents a structured autonomous multi-agent LLM-powered penetration testing framework that integrates prompt-engineered cognitive agents, Recon Strategist, Exploit Planner, and Failure Analyzer through a persistent knowledge graph and history engine. The methodology incorporates a detailed behavioral analysis of recent LLM-based PT systems, establishing taxonomies for prompt design, reasoning mechanisms, and self-reflective correction. Results demonstrate that model selection directly affects execution latency, reasoning stability, and operational cost, with GPT5 models providing strong multi-step reasoning performance at modest cost, while open-source models offer low-cost or offline alternatives with varying latency trade-offs. The proposed architecture remains portable and stable across all backends, successfully completing autonomous PT workflows in a controlled virtual environment. Overall, the study contributes a reproducible methodology, a comprehensive comparative analysis of modern LLMs for offensive security automation, and an extensible multi-agent PT framework that advances the reliability, efficiency, and ethical governance of AI-driven cybersecurity operations. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0353] year=2025 | venue=Applied Intelligence | type=Article
TITLE: Collaborative penetration testing suite for emerging generative AI algorithms
