TITLE: Leveraging Generative AI And Reinforcement Learning For Autonomous Cyber Threat Mitigation
KEYWORDS: AI for Security; Anomaly Detection; Cybersecurity; Explainable AI; Generative AI; Intrusion Detection Systems; Large Language Models; Reinforcement Learning; Threat Simulation; Zero-Day Attack Detection; Adversarial machine learning; Cybersecurity; Deep learning; Learning systems; Zero-day attack; AI for security; Anomaly detection; Attack detection; Cyber security; Explainable AI; Generative AI; Intrusion Detection Systems; Language model; Large language model; Reinforcement learnings; Threat simulation; Zero day attack; Zero-day attack detection; Anomaly detection
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity is up against attackers that are getting smarter and faster than traditional defenses. This paper proposes a new AI-driven cybersecurity framework that leverages machine learning (ML) and generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) to enhance threat detection, automate incident response, improve anomaly detection, and bolster zero-day exploit prevention. The framework solves some of the greatest concerns with AI-based security systems today, such as too many false positives, difficulty detecting new attacks, sluggish response times, and difficulty integrating. It does this by employing a structure that blends generative models (such GANs and VAEs), sophisticated deep learning, and large language model (LLM) methods. We explain how synthetic data generation and generative modeling help find unusual behavior and unknown threats before they happen, and how reinforcement learning and automation make it easier to take quick action to contain them. A proof-of-concept simulation shows that the framework could greatly improve the accuracy of detection and the speed of response compared to standard solutions. We also talk about how the framework works with tools that are already in place (like SIEM and SOAR) and keeps data private. The results indicate that employing GenAI can enhance cyber protection by making it more adaptable and intelligent. We conclude with an examination of the anticipated advantages, existing constraints (such as adversarial machine learning hazards and model interpretability), and prospective research avenues for the large-scale application of generative AI in cybersecurity. This study presents a multi-layered GenAI driven architecture that combines anomaly detection, LLM-based reasoning, and reinforcement learning to automate cybersecurity operations and minimize false positives. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0355] year=2026 | venue=Communications in Computer and Information Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SEPTA: Self-Evolving PenTester Agent
KEYWORDS: Attack Agents; Cybersecurity; Emulation; Large Language Models; Penetration Testing; Agents; Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Network security; Virtual environments; Attack agent; Cyber security; Emulation; Language model; Large language model; Levels of automation; Model-based OPC; Penetration testing; Performance; Testing process; Decision making
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity, especially the Penetration Testing process, is becoming more effective with the use of Artificial Intelligence. Large Language Models (LLMs) are now applied to various Cybersecurity tasks, improving the productivity and performance of specialists. Although LLMs play a significant role in Cybersecurity, limitations remain in the accuracy of their knowledge and the level of automation achievable by LLM-based agents. We introduce the Self-Evolving PenTester Agent (SEPTA), a system designed to evaluate and improve an agent’s knowledge of Ethical Hacking. SEPTA generates a sandbox of virtual machines and network environments for emulating a Cybersecurity context that allows safe user interaction and real-time command evaluation. We demonstrate the agent’s feasibility through a working prototype. The results show that the prototype can generate an effective decision-making process. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.

[R0356] year=2026 | venue=Expert Systems with Applications | type=Article
TITLE: CogTrust: Cognitive Logic-Based framework for dynamic trust evaluation in multi-agent systems
KEYWORDS: Agent security; Cognitive logic; Large language models; Multi-agent systems; Trust evaluation; Cognitive systems; Computational methods; Data mining; Distributed computer systems; Intelligent agents; Network security; Random processes; Risk perception; Agent security; Cognitive logic; Dynamic trusts; Language model; Large language model; Logic-based frameworks; Model-based OPC; Multiagent systems (MASs); Security risks; Trust evaluation; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: As large language models (LLMs) enhance the capabilities of multi-agent systems, they also introduce security risks that existing evaluation methods struggle to address in dynamic and complex interaction scenarios. This paper presents CogTrust, a cognitive logic-based framework for dynamic trust evaluation in LLM-based multi-agent systems. CogTrust analyzes three key dimensions of an agent’s consciousness, generation of content and actions based on cognitive logic. The framework incorporates a multi-weight subjective logic model with a time decay memory factor to balance short-term attack detection with long-term trust trends. The implementation accurately represents the trustworthiness of an agent in terms of dynamic reputation value quantification. To enhance system robustness, we embed reputation value into a resilient random perturbation defense mechanism that adapts to additive noise based on trust levels, effectively mitigating attacks while preserving system performance. Experiments conducted on popular LLM-based collaborative multi-agent systems with various LLMs demonstrate that CogTrust accurately indicates agents’ trust states, reducing the percentage of dangerous examples to 14% while maintaining a task success rate over 93%. We hope this research offers valuable insights into the research of trust systems within multi-agent environments. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.

[R0357] year=2024 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Trust, Security and Privacy in Computing and Communications, TrustCom | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Leveraging Large Language Models for Challenge Solving in Capture-the-Flag
KEYWORDS: Capture the Flag; Large Language Models; LLM Agent; Penetration Testing; Agent Framework; Capture the flag; Cyber security; Language model; Large language model; Large language model agent; Model agents; Penetration testing; Real-world
ABSTRACT: Capture-the-Flag (CTF) competitions are a prominent method in cybersecurity for practical attack and defense exercises. Despite the rapid advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs), their potential for solving CTF challenges remains underexplored. In this paper, we first propose a flexible CTF platform designed to reflect real-world penetration testing scenarios, bridging the gap between theoretical learning and practical cybersecurity challenges. Our platform is highly customizable, freely deployable, and capable of generating scenarios that closely mirror real network vulnerabilities. More importantly, we introduce an automated LLM agent framework that tackles CTF challenges using an integrated toolchain and various plugins to enhance problem-solving efficiency. In addition, we propose a human-validated LLM agent framework to address the potential limitations of the fully automated LLM agent, providing a clearer evaluation of the LLMs' intrinsic capabilities. We evaluate the agent's performance using four LLMs: GPT-4o, GPT-4o mini, o1-preview, and o1-mini. Although the LLMs' performance on dynamic and complex penetration tests reveals certain limitations, which need further exploration, our experimental results demonstrate that LLMs can leverage their extensive knowledge bases to effectively solve CTF challenges. © 2024 IEEE.

[R0358] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Cybersecurity, Digital Forensics, and AI Applications, ICCSDFAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Attacks via AI Agents: How Autonomous Systems Are Becoming the New Weapon of Cybercriminals
KEYWORDS: agentic attack chains; autonomous AI agents; cyber offense; LLM red teaming; offensive AI; operational uplift; Artificial intelligence; Computer crime; Cost engineering; Crime; Economics; Intelligent agents; Life cycle; Network security; Agentic attack chain; Autonomous AI agent; Cybe offense; Cyber security; Cybercriminals; Language model; LLM red teaming; Offensive AI; Operational uplift; Red teaming; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: Autonomous cybersecurity is evolving past the one-shot, static LLM-based attacker. What comes next is even scarier: adversarial frameworks that connect large language models to external tools, persistence and memory, and recursive task splitting/decomposition, creating fully fledged planning-and-learning systems capable of performing actions in a cyber environment, observing the results, and making new plans, almost without human intervention. Autonomous agents are not bringing any new exploit techniques; what they are doing is automating the process. Their value lies in the economic disruption - they decrease the marginal cost of an attack to zero, move attacker expertise from a scarce resource to an oversight role, and give cybercriminals the asymmetry because "good enough"autonomous output works most of the time. In this paper, we conduct a narrative review of the 2022-2026 literature on autonomous attack agents, LLM-powered red-teaming, and agent-driven attack pipelines. Our contribution includes: (i) functional decomposition of autonomous agents into cognition and executive support services, (ii) a lifecycle map including reconnaissance, social engineering, and post-compromise activities, (iii) a definition and discussion of operational uplift U = T_manual/T_AI and its implications for defender economics, and (iv) countermeasures based on the concept of the analyst bottleneck - rate limiting, automation of workflow verification, and identification of agent-generated output. The main message of our paper is that the true frontier of cyber risks lies not in a model, but in its agent system ecosystem - wrappers, plugins, and frameworks - where the transition from capability demonstration to adversarial utility is the shortest. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0359] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Open Journal of the Communications Society | type=Article
TITLE: Recent Advances in Generative Artificial Intelligence for Communication Networks
KEYWORDS: autonomous networks; communication networks; Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI); large language models (LLMs); network management; Anomaly detection; Data privacy; Data quality; Industrial management; Network security; Autonomous networks; Communications networks; Generative artificial intelligence; Language model; Large language model; Learning patterns; Networks management; New approaches; Optimisations; Security and privacy; Network management
ABSTRACT: Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) is set to transform networking by enabling new approaches to management, security and privacy. By learning patterns from existing data and generating configurations, traces and policies, GenAI enhances automation, optimisation, troubleshooting and fault resilience. Applications range from predictive modelling and natural language-based orchestration to anomaly detection, configuration optimisation and intelligent script generation. At the same time, challenges remain in data quality, explainability, robustness and compliance with privacy regulations. This paper surveys recent advances in applying GenAI to networking, structured around unifying operational patterns (Intent!Plan!Act, Predict!Allocate!Adapt, Detect!Explain!Recover). We analyse state-of-the-art techniques in network management and security, highlight their limitations and outline future research directions toward telecom-grade, resilient and trustworthy GenAI-enabled infrastructures. © 2020 IEEE.

[R0360] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the International Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work in Design, CSCWD | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Artifact-assisted Red-Team PWN Template Generation by Leveraging Large Language Model
KEYWORDS: code generation; Cybersecurity; large language model; software vulnerability; Automatic programming; Cybersecurity; Software engineering; Codegeneration; Cyber security; Generation techniques; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; Real-world; Red teaming; Software vulnerabilities; Template generation; Knowledge management
ABSTRACT: Exploiting software vulnerability from the red-teaming perspective holds significant importance for threat severity assessment. Although automatic exploit generation techniques have demonstrated their effectiveness in some specific CVEs and CTF binaries, real-world vulnerability exploitation still requires substantial experts' efforts. To address this challenge, we propose a universal method that leverages large language model (LLM) and artifacts to generate PWN (binary exploitation) templates for red-team pentesting. Firstly, we extract functionality-related semantic units and the inter-unit associations pertinent to vulnerability exploitation from existing artifacts. Subsequently, we can summarize the knowledge graph illustrating the connection among these units for exploit composition. Building upon the knowledge graph and current exploitation state, we progressively employ LLM to synthesize templates serving as intermediates for subsequent manual manipulation. Experiments demonstrate that our proposed method can provide high-quality PWN templates for memory-corruption vulnerabilities, significantly narrowing the gap in real-world software vulnerability exploitation and lowering experts' efforts. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0361] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Cybersecurity, Digital Forensics, and AI Applications, ICCSDFAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: MinervAI: Multi-LLM Powered Autonomous Cyber Threat Intelligence for Ransomware Analysis
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cyber Threat Intelligence; Knowledge Graphs; Large Language Models; MITRE ATT&CK; Ransomware; SecureBERT; SOC Automation; Threat Detection; Autonomous agents; Computation theory; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Open systems; Pipeline processing systems; Pipelines; Semantics; Agentic AI; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber threats; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; MITRE ATT&CK; Securebert; Security operation center; Security operation center automation; Threat detection; Malware
ABSTRACT: Ransomware has become one of the most sophisticated and rapidly evolving cyber threats, placing significant pressure on modern Security Operations Centers (SOCs). Analysts are required to examine massive amounts of Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) gathered from open-source feeds, technical reports, and threat-sharing platforms, yet much of this information is unstructured and difficult to process efficiently. This often increases analyst workload, slows incident response, and contributes to operational fatigue. To overcome these limitations, this study introduces MinervAI, an autonomous multi-agent CTI framework developed to automate ransomware intelligence analysis and support operational cyber defense activities. The proposed system combines LangChain ReAct agents, SecureBERT-based entity extraction, ransomware relevance detection, semantic MITRE ATT&CK mapping, and Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning within a unified intelligence pipeline. Using this architecture, the framework can automatically gather intelligence from multiple live threat sources, extract Indicators of Compromise (IoCs), identify ransomware-related activities, map attacker behaviors to MITRE ATT&CK techniques, and generate practical defense artifacts such as STIX 2.1 bundles, Sigma rules, YARA signatures, and Neo4j knowledge graphs. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves a ransomware classification accuracy of 97.99% while significantly reducing the manual effort required for CTI analysis. In addition, the complete pipeline operates with an average latency of only 47 seconds on commodity hardware without GPU acceleration, corresponding to nearly a 98% reduction in processing time compared to traditional analyst-driven workflows, while also providing full MITRE ATT&CK kill-chain coverage across all ten phases. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0362] year=2026 | venue=International Conference on Intelligent and Sustainable AI Systems, ICOSAAS 2026 - Conference Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous LLM Agent for Real-Time DDoS Attack Classification and Mitigation Rule Generation
KEYWORDS: Cyber Threat Intelligence; DDoS Detection; Large Language Models; Network Security; Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG); XGBoost Classification; Classification (of information); Computation theory; Computer system firewalls; Data flow analysis; Data visualization; Denial-of-service attack; Information retrieval; Knowledge based systems; Knowledge management; Learning systems; Machine learning; Network architecture; Search engines; Visualization; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber threats; DDoS Attack; DDoS detection; Language model; Large language model; Networks flows; Networks security; Retrieval-augmented generation; Xgboost classification; Network security
ABSTRACT: Large-scale DDoS attacks remain a serious threat to today's networked systems, which aim to make services unavailable by sending a massive amount of traffic. The traditional detection methods are mostly about attack categorization and are not that context-aware or actionable in providing support to security analysts. We propose SentinelsGuard AI, a self-learning DDoS classification and mitigation system that is built from a combination of machine learning, retrieval-augmented knowledge, and LLM-based reasoning in one platform. The proposed system is realized as a Flask-based web application, which processes the network flow statistics data, extracted from incoming traffic, and transforms the data to generate representative features of the network flows, before detecting the attacks using the XGBoost classifier. After classification, the framework accesses domain-specific attack intelligence from a structured Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) knowledge base with profiles of multiple categories of DDoS attacks. This contextual information is used with LLM-enabled reasoning to derive comprehensible security explanations, determine the severity of the attack, and suggest the firewall mitigation rules that can be applied. The framework stores analysis data in a SQLite database for operational monitoring, and displays security events in an interactive dashboard that includes traffic visualization, attack logs, and threat summaries. The architecture proposed brings statistical learning together with contextual knowledge of cybersecurity and attempts to bridge the gap between automatic attack detection and explainable incident response. It integrates all the key features of a classification-based network security decision support platform into a single stream, enabling a classification-based approach to network security to become more interpretable and usable in reality: persistent logging, visualization, knowledge retrieval, reasoning, and system recommendation of mitigations. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0363] year=2026 | venue=Electronics (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: Local LLM-Based Cyber Incident Analysis in Air-Gapped Networks via Teacher–Student Knowledge Distillation and Agentic Orchestration
KEYWORDS: artificial intelligence agent; closed network; cyber incident analysis; incident analysis; local large language model; offline knowledge distillation; Artificial intelligence; Computer crime; Computer graphics; Distillation; Distributed computer systems; Intelligent agents; Network security; Public works; Artificial intelligence agent; Closed network; Cybe incident analyze; Incident analysis; Language model; Local large language model; Model-based OPC; Offline; Offline knowledge distillation; Computational methods
ABSTRACT: Recent cyber incidents have become increasingly sophisticated through Living-off-the-Land (LotL) techniques that exploit legitimate behavior and multi-stage attacks. This requires advanced reasoning capabilities to discern the attack contexts within fragmented large-scale logs. However, closed network environments with physical network separation (air-gapped), such as national critical infrastructures, restrict the use of high-performance cloud large language models (LLMs), thereby limiting the adoption of cutting-edge artificial intelligence (AI)-based analysis technologies. To overcome these constraints, this study proposes a Local LLM-based intrusion analysis framework that operates independently within closed networks. The proposed framework combines (i) an Offline Knowledge Distillation technique that transfers the analytical reasoning process of external high-performance models to the Local LLM after a security review, and (ii) an AI agent orchestration structure that controls the analysis procedure step-by-step and suppresses hallucinations. Experiments and validation using a public dataset (Atomic Red Team) demonstrated that the proposed model achieved a consistently higher detection accuracy (88.4%) and MITRE Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge mapping performance (0.91 F1-Score) than existing general-purpose Local LLMs. Furthermore, the proposed model suppressed hallucination rates to 6.2% through an automated verification mechanism and significantly improved analysis efficiency by refining large-scale logs to focus on core events. This study quantitatively demonstrated that AI-based intrusion incident analysis can be automated using a single graphics processing unit server under controlled evaluation conditions. The proposed framework provides a practical prototype for intelligent security monitoring in closed-network environments. However, the operational performance must be validated in real-world deployments. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0364] year=2026 | venue=Journal of Supercomputing | type=Article
TITLE: Autosecagent: a semi-automated AI-driven penetration testing framework through recursive memory and real-time RAG
KEYWORDS: Automated penetration testing; Human oversight; Large language models; Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG); Security automation; Threat detection; Automation; Autonomous agents; Embeddings; Intelligent agents; Memory architecture; Multi agent systems; Network security; Remediation; Automated penetration testing; Embeddings; Human oversight; Language model; Large language model; Penetration testing; Real- time; Retrieval-augmented generation; Security automation; Threat detection; Scalability
ABSTRACT: Manual penetration testing is increasingly inadequate against rapidly evolving cyber threats, as it is time-consuming, difficult to scale, and heavily reliant on human expertise. Whilst Artificial Intelligence (AI), particularly Large Language Models (LLMs), offers automation potential, current tools suffer from poor reasoning, limited context retention, low scalability, and safety and misuse concerns. This paper introduces AutoSecAgent, an AI-powered platform designed to address these limitations. Built on the adaptive Agent Zero orchestration framework and powered by the cybersecurity-focused DeepSeek LLM, AutoSecAgent supports semi-automated end-to-end penetration testing under human oversight, covering vulnerability discovery, exploitation, and reporting with remediation guidance for follow-up. It features recursive memory embedding to maintain context across complex multi-step attack chains, supporting strategic planning and situational awareness. Additionally, real-time Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) allows AutoSecAgent to source up-to-date vulnerability data from repositories such as NVD and CVE, ensuring attack strategies incorporate current information. Its modular, agent-based architecture supports scalability across diverse network environments and integrates with tools such as Metasploit and Nmap. To avoid scattered claims across multiple design elements, this study focuses its core contributions on two tightly coupled points: (i) a memory-centric orchestration mechanism (Recursive Memory Embedding, RME) with an explicit operationalisation of context-loss errors for penetration testing trajectories and (ii) a reproducible, tool-grounded, semi-automated workflow that combines RME with real-time retrieval and a bounded online optimisation loop under human oversight. Multi-agent decomposition and DeepSeek domain tuning are treated as supporting design choices that enable these two contributions rather than separate innovations. Experimental results indicate that this design improves end-to-end performance over representative LLM-based baselines across vulnerability detection, exploitation, multi-step attack completion, and scalability in controlled evaluation settings. The empirical study is positioned as a proof-of-concept validation in controlled and semi-realistic settings. Broader operational generalisation and comparison against additional non-LLM baseline classes (e.g. scanner-only pipelines) are treated as future work. © The Author(s) 2026.


